            <ArticleTitle>Toxic epidermal necrolysis secondary to carbamazepine in a patient with human immunodeficiency virus infection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>918-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is a rare but very serious dermatologic disorder and is seen more commonly in human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infected patients. We present a case of TEN in HIV infected person secondary to carbamazepine who responded favourably to corticosteroids.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
